# g9-screen-switch: the desktop half of the phone's screen control driven by the core (the pill).

## 1. host/index.ts (DESKTOP-WIRE-G9)

New module host/screen-indicator.ts (written, with its test): `registerScreenIndicatorHost(connection, show)` serves `host/screen/indicator` and reports the
pill's Stop as `host/screen/indicator/stop {screenId}`.

    import { registerScreenIndicatorHost, type ShowScreenIndicator } from './screen-indicator';
    // the host options gain:
    showScreenIndicator: ShowScreenIndicator;
    // beside registerScreenDisplaysHost / reportScreenDisplayChanges (the returned closer is called where `stopDisplayReports` is):
    const closeScreenIndicators = registerScreenIndicatorHost(connection, options.showScreenIndicator);

index.ts (main) passes `showScreenIndicator: showRemoteScreenIndicator` (imported from './remote-screen/screen-indicator', the Electron pill that
`createPillScreenIndicator` wraps today). The protocol rows are pending-rows/g9-screen-switch.rows.

## 2. host/screen/displays is no longer asked (after the rows are applied)

The core answers the phone's `browser/screenDisplays` from its own screen sources. In host/index.ts delete the `registerScreenDisplaysHost(connection, ...)`
call and its import (keep `reportScreenDisplayChanges`); in host/screen-displays.ts delete `HOST_SCREEN_DISPLAYS`, `ScreenDisplaysSource`,
`registerScreenDisplaysHost` and the three tests of screen-displays.test.mjs that serve it (keep the display-change test); then
remote-browser-methods.ts `screenDisplays` and browser/host.ts `remoteScreenDisplays` have no caller: they go with x-del-remote-webrtc.

## 3. What is NOT switched: browser/host.ts (the Electron screen sources stay)

createRemoteScreenSources, remoteScreenAccess, remoteScreenDisplays, stopRemoteScreens and the `screens` option of `remoteWebrtc` are all still in place: the
core cannot yet carry a phone's screen viewer (see native/core/crates/pending-wire/g9-screen-switch.wire section 4: `LiveHost::signal` answers
SOURCE_NOT_STREAMED for `Source::Screen`), so removing them would remove the only implementation of the screen stream. They go, with screen-electron.ts's
sources, in the change that gives the Rust live host its screen viewer. screen-indicator*.ts, screen-geometry.ts and screen-electron.ts stay as the providers
the core asks (the pill through host/screen/indicator; the monitors through host/computer/displays and host/computer/pixels).
Until then index.ts keeps `windowsPcAccess()`, `pcAccess` and the `createScreenSourceHost` options (deferred-g3.md "x-pcaccess-desktop": the TS queue of
the phone's password path): the Electron source is what still uses them.
